s3c-hsudc: drain both OUT buffers in one interrupt

s3c_hsudc_epout_intr() read the second buffered packet only when the first
read did not complete the request. A short packet completes its request, and
nearly every ECM frame is short, so with both OUT buffers full the second
packet stayed in the FIFO until the next host packet raised another
interrupt: replies on usb0 arrived one host packet late (docs/ROBUSTNESS.md
E3, E5).

Read up to two packets per interrupt, each into the request at the head of
the queue. The giveback drops the lock and may select another endpoint, so
the index is selected again and RX_SUCCESS is re-read before every read.
While the giveback runs the endpoint is marked stopped, so a re-queue from
rx_complete only appends and never reads the FIFO itself.

Applies with -p1 to the kernel tree (.runtime/linux-trim-*).

--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/udc/s3c-hsudc.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/udc/s3c-hsudc.c
@@ -426,14 +426,26 @@
 	}
 
 	if (csr & S3C_ESR_RX_SUCCESS) {
-		if (list_empty(&hsep->queue))
-			return;
+		int n;
 
-		hsreq = list_entry(hsep->queue.next,
-				struct s3c_hsudc_req, queue);
-		if (((s3c_hsudc_read_fifo(hsep, hsreq)) == 0) &&
-				(csr & S3C_ESR_PSIF_TWO))
+		/*
+		 * Both OUT buffers can be full behind a single interrupt, and
+		 * a short packet completes its request. Drain every buffered
+		 * packet now instead of leaving the second one for the next
+		 * host packet. The giveback drops the lock and may select
+		 * another endpoint, so select ours again and trust only a
+		 * fresh status read.
+		 */
+		for (n = 0; n < 2; n++) {
+			if (list_empty(&hsep->queue))
+				return;
+			set_index(hsudc, ep_idx);
+			if (!(readl(hsudc->regs + S3C_ESR) & S3C_ESR_RX_SUCCESS))
+				return;
+			hsreq = list_entry(hsep->queue.next,
+					struct s3c_hsudc_req, queue);
 			s3c_hsudc_read_fifo(hsep, hsreq);
+		}
 	}
 }
 
